{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useRouter"},{"type":"text","value":" method is a hook that returns the current instance of "},{"type":"link","href":"./RouterType.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Router"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from context. This hook is useful for accessing the router instance in a component."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"userouter-returns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"useRouter returns"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The current "},{"type":"link","href":"./RouterType.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Router"}]},{"type":"text","value":" instance."}]}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"⚠️⚠️⚠️ "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"router.state"},{"type":"text","value":" is always up to date, but NOT REACTIVE. If you use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"router.state"},{"type":"text","value":" in a component, the component will not re-render when the router state changes. To get a reactive version of the router state, use the "},{"type":"link","href":"./useRouterStateHook.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useRouterState"}]},{"type":"text","value":" hook."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useRouter } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nfunction Component() {\n  const router = useRouter()\n  //    ^ Router\n\n  // ...\n}","lang":"tsx"}],"headings":[{"id":"userouter-returns","text":"useRouter returns","level":2},{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe `useRouter` method is a hook that returns the current instance of [`Router`](./RouterType.md) from context. This hook is useful for accessing the router instance in a component.\n\n## useRouter returns\n\n- The current [`Router`](./RouterType.md) instance.\n\n> ⚠️⚠️⚠️ **`router.state` is always up to date, but NOT REACTIVE. If you use `router.state` in a component, the component will not re-render when the router state changes. To get a reactive version of the router state, use the [`useRouterState`](./useRouterStateHook.md) hook.**\n\n## Examples\n\n```tsx\nimport { useRouter } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nfunction Component() {\n  const router = useRouter()\n  //    ^ Router\n\n  // ...\n}\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: useRouterHook\ntitle: useRouter hook\n---\n\nThe `useRouter` method is a hook that returns the current instance of [`Router`](./RouterType.md) from context. This hook is useful for accessing the router instance in a component.\n\n## useRouter returns\n\n- The current [`Router`](./RouterType.md) instance.\n\n> ⚠️⚠️⚠️ **`router.state` is always up to date, but NOT REACTIVE. If you use `router.state` in a component, the component will not re-render when the router state changes. To get a reactive version of the router state, use the [`useRouterState`](./useRouterStateHook.md) hook.**\n\n## Examples\n\n```tsx\nimport { useRouter } from '@tanstack/react-router'\n\nfunction Component() {\n  const router = useRouter()\n  //    ^ Router\n\n  // ...\n}\n```\n"}
